  "notes": "The producer of symphonic/goth pop-rock — the piano-plus-down-tuned-guitar formula. The singles sit 88–97 straight with the backbeat on 2&4 and the ballads at 57–74, both straight, so a single window carries 57–97. Harmony is E minor and other natural minors over i–VI–III–VII and i–iv–VI, \"piano-voiced with minor-add9; **no dominant, no modulation**\", so no authored family contains a V, the ninth share is high, and the voicing is close because a piano voices it. Three drum deltas are quoted: \"kick 1 and the '&' of 2 with double-kick 16ths under the riff\", \"snare 2&4 in a large room\", and \"china-crash accents\" with \"timpani and orchestral bass drum used as fills\". What separates this model from the Evanescence artist entry that shares its records is the process the research documents on the producer side: **iterative demoing** — \"roughly ten demo versions of the flagship before the master\" — which reads as a tighter grid and a narrower velocity spread than a band tracking live, and the **22-piece string section recorded separately** at a film stage, which is why the counter carries a sustained pad alongside the answer lick and runs at a higher ratio than the artist model's. The bass is drop-tuned and kick-locked with the piano's left hand doubling it an octave up, so the chord voicing is lifted clear of the bass ceiling. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are every drum probability; the string-section size, arranger, demo process, mix console and the flagship's key and tempo are sourced, the values are not.",
